hyperparameter posterior against the proposal the integrator fits (mixed
per-axis transforms: \code{log} for positive scales, logit for the BYM2 mixing
weight, identity for the copy coefficient \eqn{\alpha}). \code{k_samples}
is the number of importance draws, each one an extra inner joint solve, and
is the diagnostic's precision knob: a tighter k-hat needs MORE actual tail
ratios, so increase \code{k_samples} (not \code{k_bootstrap}). The draws are
RNG-restored so the fit's modes / draws are unchanged. A fit carrying an
axis whose support is not safely known (CAR_proper's \code{rho_car}) declines to
the quadrature-ESS fallback (\code{pareto_k = NA}). \code{FALSE} skips the diagnostic.
\code{"by_arm"} additionally computes a k-hat restricted to each arm's
hyperparameter axes (the other arms held at their posterior mean), reported
in \code{pareto_k_by_arm}, to localise which arm drives a tail-heavy joint k; the
joint k itself is unchanged. Per-arm k is defined for the multi-block layout
with two or more arms and declines for the single-block shared-field layout.
The legacy \code{k_samples} name is accepted as an alias for \code{k_samples}.
\item \code{k_threads} (\code{NULL}) -- outer-thread width for the diagnostic's importance
batch. The \code{k_samples} re-solves are independent and run after the grid
(every core free), each solved single-threaded once the batch saturates the
pool, so widening it is a bit-identical wall-clock speedup (the k-hat is
unchanged). \code{NULL} follows the fit's own thread grant -- the larger of
\code{n_threads_outer} and the inner \code{n_threads} -- so a serial fit keeps a serial
diagnostic while a threaded fit gets a free parallel one. \code{"auto"} uses the
physical performance-core count (capped at 2 under R CMD check); an integer
pins the width (\code{1L} forces serial). Always capped at \code{k_samples}.
\item \code{k_bootstrap} (\code{1000L}) -- bootstrap replicates for the outer
Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}} uncertainty. The k-hat is a single fixed number for a
fit + proposal; its sampling uncertainty GIVEN the proposal is estimated by
resampling the diagnostic's raw importance log-ratios with replacement and
re-fitting the GPD tail \code{k_bootstrap} times (no new inner solves). Reports
\code{pareto_k_se_boot} (bootstrap SE), \code{pareto_k_ci_low} / \code{pareto_k_ci_high}
(the 2.5\\% / 97.5\\% bootstrap quantiles), \code{pareto_k_se_formula} (the
closed-form GPD-shape MLE asymptotic SE \eqn{(1 + k)/\sqrt{M}}, a
cross-check), and \code{pareto_k_band_confident} (TRUE iff the bootstrap CI lies
within one reliability band). The bootstrap measures how UNSTABLE the
current tail estimate is; it cannot create tail information. Increase
\code{k_samples}, not \code{k_bootstrap}, to obtain more tail information. \code{0L}
skips it (point k-hat only). The per-arm k carries the same fields.
\item \code{k_tail_points} (\code{NULL}) -- number of upper-tail order statistics for the
GPD fit. \code{NULL} uses the automatic PSIS rule
\eqn{\lceil\min(0.2 N, 3\sqrt{N})\rceil}. An explicit value is an EXPERT
tail-threshold control, capped at the 20\\%-of-draws ceiling (with a warning)
so the fit stays an extreme tail; it is NOT a precision knob (a request that
drags body ratios into the tail lowers variance but biases the k-hat). The
used and requested counts are reported in \code{pareto_k_tail_points} /
\code{pareto_k_tail_points_requested}.
\item \code{k_conf_bands} (\code{NULL}) -- the reliability-band boundaries the bootstrap CI
is tested against for \code{pareto_k_band_confident}. \code{NULL} (default) uses the
sample-size-dependent boundaries \eqn{c(0.5, \min(1 - 1/\log_{10} S, 0.7))}
at the realised draw count \eqn{S} (Vehtari et al. 2024): the good cut is
0.5 and the usable cut tightens below 0.7 for small \eqn{S} (about 0.565 at
\eqn{S = 200}, reaching 0.7 only past \eqn{S \approx 2154}). Supply a
strictly-increasing numeric vector to fix the boundaries instead, e.g.
\code{c(0.5, 0.7)} for the size-independent good / ok / unreliable split.
\item \code{diagnose_skew} (\code{TRUE}), \code{skew_idx} (\code{NULL}) -- compute the
inner-Laplace skewness diagnostic (\verb{$inner_skew}, gamma_3, Rue Martino
& Chopin 2009 Sec 3.2.3) at the fitted MAP grid cell: one extra Newton
solve via the same \code{kernel_fn} the outer diagnostic reuses, scoring
every arm's fixed-effects coefficients by default (pass \code{skew_idx},
1-based indices in the joint \verb{[arm1_beta | arm1_re | arm2_beta | ... | blocks]} latent layout -- see \verb{$arm_layout} -- to probe additional
indices). This is the complementary layer to \code{diagnose_k}: that scores
the outer hyperparameter-grid integration around a FIXED inner
Laplace, this scores whether that inner Gaussian approximation is
itself a good fit. A genuinely coupled arm (\code{cell_coupling != "separable"} on that arm, e.g. tulpaObs's \code{occu_cover}) has no per-obs
likelihood for the separable formula to score; it is scored instead by
the contraction of the cell third-derivative tensor
(gcol33/tulpa#301), which differences the cross-arm Hessian the coupling
spec already returns. A fit that can carry neither reports \code{NaN}, not a
silently wrong 0, and \verb{$inner_skew_declined} says WHY -- \code{"coupled_arm"}
marks arms the inner layer could not score at all, distinct from a
diagnostic that was simply switched off (gcol33/tulpa#296) -- while
\verb{$inner_skew_arms_declined} names the arms with no oracle, including on a
partially scored fit. See
\code{\link[=diagnostics]{diagnostics()}} for the combined verdict. Wired
for both the single-block backends (icar/bym2/car_proper) and the
multi-block path (a per-group RE, a trend field, or an arm-specific
field block).
\item \code{skew_correct} (\code{FALSE}) -- consume \code{gamma_3} instead of only grading it
(gcol33/tulpa#302): report Cornish-Fisher marginal quantiles at each
coefficient's own \code{gamma_3} from \code{summary()} / \code{confint()}, wherever the
combined inner band (\code{gamma_3} and the inner importance k-hat) says the
leading-order expansion is in its regime, and the Gaussian quantiles
everywhere else. The correction reshapes where the interval sits and
leaves the centre and the draws untouched, so a fit run with it off is bit
for bit the fit it was before. \verb{$skew_correction} records the
per-coefficient \code{gamma_3}, its band, the k-hat, the combined band, the
eligibility and the reason behind it; the \code{skew_applied} attribute on
\code{summary()} / \code{confint()} records what was actually used at the requested
level. It stays \code{FALSE}: scored over the whole marginal rather than at a
symmetric endpoint pair, the correction is a net loss, because the term it
applies is a pure location shift at a symmetric pair and the location term
that would put the centre in the right place is not computed here
(gcol33/tulpa#346; see \code{\link[=tulpa_nested_laplace]{tulpa_nested_laplace()}}).
\item \code{subspace_debias} (\code{FALSE}) -- correct only the latent directions the
inner-layer diagnostics flagged, by exact Metropolis along the
Gaussian-conditional-mean surface through each cell's mode, and leave the
rest at their Gaussian conditional (gcol33/tulpa#304, extended to both
joint paths by gcol33/tulpa#306). Settings and semantics are the ones
documented on \code{\link[=tulpa_nested_laplace]{tulpa_nested_laplace()}}. On a fully coupled fit \code{gamma_3}
is \code{NaN} for every arm, so the selector rests on the derivative-free
inner Pareto-k-hat (gcol33/tulpa#303); where that also bands the
coordinate reliable, \code{idx} pins the set explicitly. An EMPTY selection
leaves the fit bit-for-bit identical to the plain path. A fit whose
inner solve took the s2z rank-1 or the PSD eigen-clamp path carries no
usable factor to build the surface from and is left uncorrected, the same
two paths \code{diagnose_skew} declines on.
\item \code{auto_recenter} (\code{TRUE}) -- re-centre a default outer grid axis on its
posterior mode and refit when the fit rails against a boundary node
(gcol33/tulpa#289 / #290). \code{FALSE} integrates over the grid exactly as
given, whatever it is, and records
\code{outer_grid_recenter_declined = "auto_recenter_disabled"}.
\item \code{max_grid_cells} (\code{2048L}) -- cell-count ceiling on a multi-block tensor
outer grid, refused with an error above it. Each cell is one inner
Newton solve, so the default catches per-block grids that multiplied out
to a run nobody asked for; a deliberate converged tensor reference grid
(4 axes at 7 levels is 2401 cells) raises it here, which \code{integration = "ccd"} cannot serve since a CCD is a different integration design.
\item \code{checkpoint} (\code{NULL}) -- grid-cell checkpoint/resume. Set
\code{list(path = "fit.ckpt", resume = TRUE)} to make a killed or interrupted
fit resumable: each completed outer-grid cell is appended to \code{path}, and
a later call with the same responses + grid + control loads the finished
cells and solves only the rest. EVA-scale joint fits run for hours, so a
wrapper teardown, reboot, or OOM near the end otherwise loses the whole
run. \code{resume = TRUE} (the default when a \code{path} is given) continues from
an existing file; \code{resume = FALSE} removes it first and starts over.
Adaptive-grid refinement cells are checkpointed under their own
coordinate keys, so resume covers them too. A file written for different
data or solver settings is rejected (fingerprint mismatch) rather than
resumed onto a stale result.
}}
}
\value{
A list of class \code{c("tulpa_nested_laplace_joint", "tulpa_nested_laplace", "list")} with:
\itemize{
\item \code{theta_grid}, \code{theta_names} -- outer-grid hyperparameter values
(includes the \code{alpha} axis when \code{copy} is set).
\item \code{log_marginal}, \code{weights} -- per-grid-point log-marginal and integration
weights (sum to 1).
\item \code{theta_mean}, \code{theta_sd} -- posterior moments per hyperparameter,
including \code{alpha} when \code{copy} is set.
\item \code{theta_median}, \code{theta_ci_lo}, \code{theta_ci_hi} -- weighted-quantile
median and 2.5/97.5 empirical CI per hyperparameter axis (same
names as \code{theta_mean}). Recommended summary for right-skewed
scale-like axes (alpha at small n_pos, sigma/range/phi near
a boundary), where the posterior mean is pulled by the right
tail away from the bulk and \verb{mean +/- 1.96 sd} mis-states the
uncertainty.
\item \code{modes} -- \verb{[n_grid x n_x]} matrix of inner modes.
\item \code{n_iter} -- inner Newton iterations per grid point.
\item \code{arm_layout} -- list with per-arm \code{beta_start}, \code{re_start},
spatial offset(s) and \code{n_x} for decoding modes.
\item \code{prior}, \code{responses}, \code{copy} -- echoed inputs.
\item \code{timing} -- named numeric of wall-clock seconds: \code{total} plus the
\code{setup} (validation / encoding / grid construction), \code{grid} (inner
Laplace solves, including adaptive-refinement and consistency passes),
\code{postproc} (weight / moment / marginal assembly) and \code{diagnostics}
(outer Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}}) phases. The \code{grid} phase is the one that
scales with grid size and core count. Surfaced one-line in \code{print}.
\item \code{pareto_k}, \code{pareto_k_is_ess}, \code{pareto_k_scope} -- outer
Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}} accuracy diagnostic and its importance-sampling
ESS (both \code{NA} when \code{control$diagnose_k = FALSE} or the fit declines;
see the \code{diagnose_k} control knob). \code{pareto_k < 0.7} indicates the
nested integration is reliable; \verb{>= 0.7} that the (skewed / heavy-
tailed) hyperparameter posterior is misfit by the Gaussian grid and
the fit should escalate to an exact debias.
\item \code{pareto_k_declined} -- when \code{pareto_k} is \code{NA}, WHY (gcol33/tulpa#295):
\code{"not_requested"} (\code{control$diagnose_k = FALSE}; nothing is wrong),
\code{"unguessable_axis: <axis>"} (a support the engine will not guess, e.g.
car_proper's \code{rho_car} -- a PERMANENT limitation of that family, so read
the quadrature ESS instead), \code{"draws_too_few"}, \code{"grid_too_small"},
\code{"no_varying_axis"}, \code{"degenerate_proposal"} (the outer mode curvature
came back non-finite -- a signal about the fit), \code{"not_applicable"}, or
\code{"internal_inconsistency"} (an engine bug worth reporting). \code{NA} on a
fit whose \eqn{\hat{k}} WAS computed.
\item \code{pareto_k_proposal_source} -- how the outer importance proposal the
\eqn{\hat{k}} scores was built: \code{"mode_hessian"} from the Laplace
curvature at the hyperparameter mode (the CCD design's, or a
finite-difference Hessian when a sharp posterior collapses the grid),
\code{"grid_moment"} from the grid-weighted covariance, \code{"moment_matched"}
when the moment-matching refinement improved on either, \code{"grid_mixture"}
when the faithful mixture-over-grid-cells proposal won, or
\code{"skew_normal"} when the skew-normal rescue did. \code{NA} when the
diagnostic is off or declines. The mode-Hessian source keeps the
\eqn{\hat{k}} meaningful when the grid concentrates on ~1 cell.
\item \code{pareto_k_regime} -- whether the outer grid actually integrated
hyperparameter uncertainty: \code{"spread"}, or \code{"collapsed_interior"} /
\code{"collapsed_edge"} when the quadrature effective sample size falls below
two cells so the integration has degenerated to a point evaluation at
the modal hyperparameter. An interior collapse is benign (the grid
bracketed the mode; the fit is empirical Bayes at that mode and only the
integrated hyperparameter uncertainty is missing); an edge collapse means
the mode sits at an extreme node, so the grid may be too narrow.
Attached from the stored weights, so it is present even with
\code{control$diagnose_k = FALSE}.
\item \code{pareto_k_grid_edge_axes}, \code{pareto_k_grid_edge_sides} -- for an edge
collapse, which axes the dominant cell sits against and on which side
(\code{"lower"} / \code{"upper"}); widen those axes and refit to confirm the mode
is bracketed. Axes the grid pins to one value are excluded (pinned, not
at a boundary).
\item \code{outer_grid_placement} -- \code{"fixed"} (the default \code{sigma_grid} axis was
used as-is) or \code{"auto_recentered"} when a \code{collapsed_edge} on \code{sigma}
triggered the mode-Hessian recenter-and-refit (gcol33/tulpa#289; see
the \code{prior} argument above). \code{outer_grid_recenter_attempts} (integer)
and \code{outer_grid_prior_added} (logical: whether the light default
PC(U=3, alpha=0.01) sigma prior was applied) are set alongside it, plus
\code{outer_grid_prior_declined = "prior_pinned"} when a second attempt ran
but the caller's pinned \code{prior_sigma} held that prior back
(gcol33/tulpa#297).
\item \code{outer_grid_recenter_declined} -- on a \code{"fixed"} placement, why the
recenter did not run: \code{"grid_not_collapsed"} (the grid already brackets
the mode -- the common case, no refit needed), \code{"axis_pinned"} (the
caller pinned \code{sigma_grid}; mark it with \code{\link[=auto_grid]{auto_grid()}} if it is a
default rather than a choice), or \code{"no_usable_curvature"} (the
mode-Hessian the recenter needs was unavailable or degenerate, e.g. a
car_proper grid whose \code{rho_car} axis has unguessable support). Absent
when the fit WAS recentred.
\item \code{pareto_k_outer_skew} -- per-axis skewness of the hyperparameter marginal
in the proposal's whitened coordinate, present only when a
\eqn{\hat{k}} above the good band triggered the skew-normal rescue pass.
It is the EXPLANATION for an inflated \eqn{\hat{k}}: a symmetric Gaussian
proposal against a right-skewed variance-component marginal has a heavy
importance-ratio tail whatever the integration's quality. \code{NULL} when the
Gaussian proposal already fit.
\item \code{pareto_k_se_boot}, \code{pareto_k_ci_low}, \code{pareto_k_ci_high},
\code{pareto_k_se_formula}, \code{pareto_k_tail_points},
\code{pareto_k_tail_points_requested}, \code{pareto_k_band_confident} -- the outer
Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}} uncertainty, present whenever the
diagnostic ran. \code{pareto_k_se_boot} is the bootstrap SE of the k-hat and
\code{pareto_k_ci_low} / \code{pareto_k_ci_high} its 2.5\\% / 97.5\\% bootstrap
quantiles -- the estimator's sampling spread GIVEN the proposal, not a
posterior credible interval; \code{pareto_k_se_formula} is the closed-form
GPD-shape MLE asymptotic SE cross-check. \code{pareto_k_tail_points} is the GPD
tail size used and \code{pareto_k_tail_points_requested} the \code{k_tail_points}
request (\code{NA} when automatic). \code{pareto_k_band_confident} is TRUE iff the
bootstrap CI lies within one reliability band, \code{NA} when the bootstrap was
off or could not fit. \code{diagnose_draws} and \code{diagnose_cost_ratio} (the
diagnostic's draw budget and its wall-clock cost relative to the fit) are
attached at the top level.
\item \code{pareto_k_by_arm}, \code{pareto_k_by_arm_is_ess}, \code{pareto_k_by_arm_scope} --
present only with \code{control$diagnose_k = "by_arm"}.
Named (by arm) outer Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}} restricted to each arm's
hyperparameter axes, the other arms held at their posterior mean, so a
tail-heavy joint k can be localised to one arm. A per-arm entry is \code{NA}
when that arm carries no varying axis. Each arm carries its own bootstrap
uncertainty: \code{pareto_k_by_arm_se_boot}, \code{pareto_k_by_arm_ci_low} /
\code{pareto_k_by_arm_ci_high}, \code{pareto_k_by_arm_se_formula},
\code{pareto_k_by_arm_tail_points} and \code{pareto_k_by_arm_band_confident}.
\item \code{k_quality_requested}, \code{k_quality_reached}, \code{k_quality_best},
\code{k_quality_reason}, \code{k_quality_rounds} -- the reliability verdict for the
\code{control$k_quality} intent. \code{k_quality_requested}
echoes the intent; \code{k_quality_best} is the band actually achieved
(\code{"good"} / \code{"ok"} / \code{"unreliable"}, or \code{"uncertain"} when the bootstrap CI
crosses a boundary); \code{k_quality_reached} is \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE} for an \code{"ok"} /
\code{"good"} target (\code{NA} for \code{"report"} / \code{"none"}), never silently
downgraded; \code{k_quality_reason} records why it stopped; and
\code{k_quality_rounds} is the number of escalation re-fits performed (\code{0} when
the first fit sufficed or escalation was off).
\item \code{adaptive_grid_info} -- when \code{adaptive_grid = TRUE}, a list with
\code{triggered_axes} (character) and \code{n_points_added} (integer)
describing the refinement passes. NULL otherwise.
\item \code{local_ccd_info} -- when \code{local_ccd} engaged (or \code{k_refine = "ccd"}), a
list with \code{n_cells_refined}, \code{n_nodes_added}, the refined \code{cells}, the
\code{n_cells_before} / \code{n_cells_after} grid sizes, \code{n_design_nodes} (the cells
that carry an in-cell design weight rather than their own mass),
\code{design_mass} (the share of the integration weight sitting on them) and
\code{cell_share} (the share each refined cell held on the BASE grid, before
any node was placed). The two shares differ: the replacement nodes sit
nearer the peak than the cell's own coordinate did, so refining raises
the share the refined region holds, and reading both separates how
concentrated the base grid already was from how much the refinement
concentrated it. Three per-cell readings come with it, on three
orthogonal axes, none of them gating anything. Shape: \code{misfit} (the
standardized cubic magnitude of the part of the cell the design cannot
represent, the one reading \code{skew_max} gates on, with \code{misfit_declined} /
\code{cells_declined} for the cells put back as their own mass atoms).
Centring: \code{offset} (the norm of the whitened gradient, i.e. how far the
cell's own peak sat from the cell's coordinate in units of its marginal
spread) and \code{mode_gain} (the same displacement in the cell's own
curvature units, \verb{0.5 * g' (-H)^-1 g} in nats, read off the same fit's
quadratic coefficients and comparable across cells whose curvature
differs; \code{NA} where \code{-H} is not positive definite, a cell with no
interior peak to be displaced from). Mass: \code{log_mass_ratio} (the cell's
refined mass over the coarse atom it replaced, \code{log_mass_refined - log_mass_coarse}, both carried on the log scale with the cell's outer
design weight in them) and \code{max_node_weight} (the share the single
largest node takes of its own cell's refined mass). Each carries a
\verb{_declined} twin for the cells the gate put back, whose nodes were
evaluated before the score was read. \code{misfit} certifies that the design
can represent the cell's shape and nothing more: a cell can be exactly
quadratic and still be read at a point that is not representative of it
(\code{offset} / \code{mode_gain}), and refining it can still move how much mass it
competes for against its unrefined neighbours (\code{log_mass_ratio}, the
embedded-rule local error indicator of adaptive cubature). NULL when
local CCD was off or declined (single-block, \verb{< 4} axes, an active
\code{phi_grid}, or no peaked interior cell).
\item \code{integration_requested}, \code{integration_declined} -- what \code{integration}
asked for, and why the CCD did not run. \verb{$integration} names the
integrator that RAN, and \code{.nl_node_support()} keys the interval
construction off it, so a caller who asked for a moment rule and
received a density grid reads the reason here rather than inferring it.
\code{integration_declined} is \code{NA_character_} when nothing was declined, and
otherwise one of \code{"axis_count"} (fewer transformable latent axes than the
requested mode's threshold), \code{"unguessable_axis"} (a CAR_proper
\code{rho_car} or a non-BYM2 \code{rho}), \code{"degenerate_axis"} (a single-valued
axis), \code{"modefind_ridge"} / \code{"modefind_boundary"} /
\code{"modefind_degenerate"} / \code{"modefind_failed"}, \code{"hessian_singular"} or
\code{"hessian_not_pd"}. Multi-block fits only.
\item \code{weight_kind} -- one entry per outer-grid cell, \code{"mass"} or \code{"design"}.
A tensor cell holds the mass of its own cell and a CCD node holds a
design weight, so a fit integrated by one rule reports one value
throughout; a locally refined grid carries both, and this says which per
cell instead of leaving it to be read off \code{integration}.
\item \code{theta_interval_read}, \code{theta_interval_design_mass} -- what the reported
per-axis \code{theta_median} / \code{theta_ci_lo} / \code{theta_ci_hi} were read off, and
how much of the integration weight sits on nodes whose cumulative sum is
not a CDF. \code{"density"} is a weighted quantile over cells that discretize
the posterior (\code{design_mass} 0), \code{"moment_rule"} an interval from the
moments a central-composite design delivers (\code{design_mass} 1), and
\code{"mixed"} the locally CCD-refined grid, which carries both kinds at once.
A mixed support still reports the weighted quantile, which measured best
against a converged reference in both \code{design_mass} regimes; the share is
the regime variable, since on that part of the support the quantile is
bounded by the refined cells' own grid neighbourhoods rather than by the
posterior (gcol33/tulpa#317).
\item \code{prune_cheap_log_marginal}, \code{prune_mask}, \code{prune_n_pruned},
\code{prune_tol} -- present only when \code{prune = TRUE} and the safety gate did
not fall back. Cheap-pass log-marginals at every cell, a logical mask
of pruned cells, the pruned-cell count, and the threshold actually
applied. Pruned cells have \code{log_marginal = -Inf} so they get zero
weight under \code{.nl_normalise_weights_safe}.
\item \code{prune_fallback_triggered}, \code{prune_fallback_reason} -- present only when
the safety gate fell back to the full grid. The returned fit is the
full-grid (unpruned) result; the reason string records which gate
condition tripped.
}
}
\description{
Outer-grid nested Laplace driver for \emph{joint} models -- multiple response
arms sharing one latent prior block, parameterized as a per-arm field
amplitude (sigma) on a unit-precision latent.

Supported priors:
\itemize{
\item \code{"bym2"}       -- outer grid over \verb{(sigma, rho [, alpha])}.
Latent: \code{phi (n_s) | theta (n_s)} with unit-precision
ICAR + iid components.
\item \code{"icar"}       -- outer grid over \code{(sigma [, alpha])}.
Latent: \code{phi (n_s)} with unit-precision ICAR.
\item \code{"car_proper"} -- outer grid over \verb{(sigma, rho_car [, alpha])}.
Latent: \code{phi (n_s)} with \code{Q = D - rho_car * W}.
}

Other backends (NNGP, HSGP, RW1/2, AR1) follow the same interface and
land under Phase 3.
}
\section{(sigma, alpha) parameterization}{

Each arm's linear predictor reads
\deqn{\eta_{arm} = X_{arm} \beta_{arm} + \sigma_{arm} \cdot z_s,}
where \eqn{z_s} is a unit-precision latent (ICAR(tau=1) for ICAR/BYM2,
or the BYM2 mix; CAR_proper uses the structure of
\eqn{D - \rho_{car} W}). All arms share the donor amplitude
\eqn{\sigma} from the outer-grid \code{sigma_grid} axis. The copy arm's
amplitude is \eqn{\sigma_{arm} = \alpha \cdot \sigma}, where \eqn{\alpha}
is a direct outer-grid axis taken from \code{copy$alpha_grid}. The Cartesian
product is over \verb{(sigma, [rho/rho_car,] alpha[, phi])}. Direct \eqn{\alpha}
as an outer-grid axis (rather than a post-hoc ratio
\eqn{\alpha = \sigma_{pos} / \sigma_{occ}}) avoids plug-in bias on the
weakly-identified ratio at small \code{n_pos} and lets a regularizing
hyperprior land on \eqn{\alpha} directly.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
set.seed(1)
S <- 25L                                   # shared spatial units (chain graph)
nb <- lapply(seq_len(S), function(s) setdiff(c(s - 1L, s + 1L), c(0L, S + 1L)))
nn <- lengths(nb)
field <- as.numeric(scale(cumsum(rnorm(S, 0, 0.4))))
mk_arm <- function(m, fam) {
  si <- sample(S, m, replace = TRUE); x <- rnorm(m)
  lin <- 0.2 + 0.5 * x + 0.8 * field[si]
  y <- if (fam == "binomial") rbinom(m, 1L, plogis(lin)) else lin + rnorm(m, 0, 0.5)
  list(y = as.numeric(y), n_trials = rep(1L, m), X = cbind(1, x),
       spatial_idx = si, family = fam, phi = if (fam == "gaussian") 0.5 else 1)
}
prior <- list(type = "icar", n_spatial_units = S,
              adj_row_ptr = c(0L, cumsum(nn)), adj_col_idx = unlist(nb) - 1L,
              n_neighbors = nn, sigma_grid = c(0.3, 0.7, 1.4))
fit <- tulpa_nested_laplace_joint(
  responses = list(occ = mk_arm(200L, "binomial"), pos = mk_arm(200L, "gaussian")),
  prior = prior)
fit$theta_mean        # shared field amplitude, integrated across both arms
}
}
\references{
Rue, Martino & Chopin (2009). Approximate Bayesian inference for latent
Gaussian models by using integrated nested Laplace approximations.
\emph{JRSS-B} 71(2):319-392.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=tulpa_nested_laplace]{tulpa_nested_laplace()}} for the single-arm engine.
}
